<p>
  Every other anole you see in Miami was brought here by people, deliberately or
  by accident, from islands across the Caribbean. The green anole got here on its own,
  and a very long time ago: its ancestors crossed from Cuba to Florida roughly 5 to
  7 million years ago, and the species now reaches as far north as Tennessee and
  North Carolina, far beyond the tropics where its relatives live.
</p>
<p>
  Its color-changing trick earned it the nickname &ldquo;American chameleon,&rdquo; a
  misnomer that has mostly fallen out of use. Green anoles eat mainly small
  insects, although they have also been seen licking nectar from flowers. They
  will take bigger prey when it comes along: in August 2018 we caught an adult male
  in the University of Miami's Gifford Arboretum part-way through swallowing a
  juvenile African house gecko.
</p>

<h2>Where did all the green anoles go?</h2>
<p>
  People who grew up in the southeast often remember green lizards on the porch
  that seem scarcer now. They are not imagining it, but the story is not one of
  simple replacement. Where brown anoles arrive, green anoles tend to move upward
  into the canopy rather than disappear, so the lizards are often still there, just
  above eye level.
</p>

<h2>A tropical lizard in a temperate world</h2>
<p>
  How does a lizard from tropical Cuba end up living through Tennessee winters?
  That question sits at the center of our work on green anoles at Georgia Tech,
  which happens to be perfectly placed along the species' range. Our initial work
  suggests that changes in physiological cold tolerance are not what made the
  range expansion possible: lower thermal limits differ surprisingly little across
  the range, especially set against how much the climate itself varies. Behavior
  seems to be what changes. Where winter air temperatures drop below what green
  anoles can tolerate, they stop living only in trees and move down into
  sheltered refuges underground, sidestepping the cold rather than evolving to
  withstand it.
</p>
<p>
  We follow green anoles through the seasons, counting them at night for our
  Sleepy Lizard Project (57 lizards in 60 minutes on the first survey of fall 2026)
  and, on the Georgia Tech campus in Atlanta, fitting some with ultra-lightweight wearable sensors that record
  movement and behavior on free-living animals. Green anoles were also one of six
  species whose cold tolerance shifted after south Florida's extreme cold snap in
  January 2020, a story told on our
  <a href="/research/cold-snaps/">cold snaps page</a>.
</p>
